{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-74320" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/assets/a9f9418d0a9984af019254cb06d9b2aab3251dd3127f05b89b118620ac6dd7e2.png)\n\nWhat are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-74320" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where $\\rho \\ge 0,$ $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ and $0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $A$ equals $2$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $A$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $A$ forms an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $A$ are $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-74320" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a11863d4aedb343c63cde88c7118ffce3447ea0bcbacd24766d7f9980e695526"} {"question" "q-74320" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-74320" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(2,-\\frac{\\pi }{3},-\\frac{\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})"] ["math" "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},-\\frac{\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{3})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-74320" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-165340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/assets/57ce4048bd9357072fadcee7fb65c5e9c2823572ec48b8bb04fbaad3c464aa3d.png)\n\nWhat are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $Q?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-165340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where $\\rho \\ge 0,$ $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ and $0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $Q$ equals $3$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $Q$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{14}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $Q$ forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $Q$ are $(3,\\frac{5\\pi }{14},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-165340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a11863d4aedb343c63cde88c7118ffce3447ea0bcbacd24766d7f9980e695526"} {"question" "q-165340" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-165340" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(3,\\frac{5\\pi }{14},\\frac{2\\pi }{3})"] ["math" "(3,\\frac{5\\pi }{14},\\frac{\\pi }{3})"] ["math" "(3,\\frac{6\\pi }{7},\\frac{\\pi }{3})"] ["math" "(3,\\frac{\\pi }{14},\\frac{\\pi }{3})"] ["math" "(3,\\frac{\\pi }{7},\\frac{2\\pi }{3})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-165340" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(3,\\frac{5\\pi }{14},\\frac{2\\pi }{3})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-165301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/assets/0f89beaaf82692e398a3259140b735feb925395c6e6d9f63b72a5406d0d5073f.png)\n\nWhat are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $Q?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-165301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where $\\rho \\ge 0,$ $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ and $0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $Q$ equals $\\sqrt{2}$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $Q$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{8}=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $Q$ forms an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $Q$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-165301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a11863d4aedb343c63cde88c7118ffce3447ea0bcbacd24766d7f9980e695526"} {"question" "q-165301" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-165301" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{8},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{4})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-165301" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-74542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/assets/fe4ff907283c9fbce1907651c4047625d750eddfc75f9a9c34f845c3a92b6036.png)\n\nWhat are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-74542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that we write the position of a point in spherical coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where $\\rho \\ge 0,$ $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ and $0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $P$ equals $4$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $P$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $P$ forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are $(4,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-74542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a11863d4aedb343c63cde88c7118ffce3447ea0bcbacd24766d7f9980e695526"} {"question" "q-74542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-74542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{6})"] ["math" "(4,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{3})"] ["math" "(4,\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\pi }{3})"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{3})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-74542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(4,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{3})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-74543" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/assets/561430ec00f405b6cce3ab743de1665fce8909f0a505bcd0df05478badafae35.png)\n\nWhat are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $D?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-74543" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where $\\rho \\ge 0,$ $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ and $0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $D$ equals $2$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $D$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $D$ forms an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $D$ are $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-74543" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a11863d4aedb343c63cde88c7118ffce3447ea0bcbacd24766d7f9980e695526"} {"question" "q-74543" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-74543" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "(4,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{-\\pi }{4})"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{4})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-74543" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-74554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The point $H$ has spherical polar coordinates $(4,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $H.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-74554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $H$ are $(1,\\sqrt{3},-2\\sqrt{3}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-74554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a11863d4aedb343c63cde88c7118ffce3447ea0bcbacd24766d7f9980e695526"} {"question" "q-74554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-74554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-3,\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{3})"] ["math" "(-\\sqrt{3},2\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{3})"] ["math" "(1,\\sqrt{3},-2\\sqrt{3})"] ["math" "(1,\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{3})"] ["math" "(3,-2\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{3})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-74554" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(1,\\sqrt{3},-2\\sqrt{3})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-74551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The point $B$ has spherical coordinates $(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-74551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(0,\\sqrt{3},3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037850/state.json" "file_sha256" "1dec5651a26cc76a7511d3072282061bf93df7fee4bebcd65895c70488438f4f"} {"question" "q-74551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/1403785